  "id": "chunk_23",
  "chunk": "when ‘thinking’ is enabled across all three providers. Although Figure 1 does\nnot show the difference between ‘non-thinking’ and ‘thinking’ for Gemini and\nChatGPT, we found similar results as using Claude, i.e. minimal differences.\nRegarding Claude, Opus 4.5 seems to slightly outperform Sonnet 4.5, but ‘think-\ning’ did not result in increased performance. These results suggest that what is\nlabelled as ’thinking’ does not translate to measurable improvements in game-\nplay performance, raising questions about whether these mechanisms capture\nthe adaptive, metacognitive processes that enable human players to learn and\nimprove at games likeZork.\nAlthough the number of moves does not say much about the ability to play\nthe game, the right-hand side of Figure 1 provides some insight in when the game\nended. Recall that the game ends when (i) the maximum score is achieved, (ii)\nthe player dies, or (iii) the LLM provides the command ‘I give up’. Interestingly,",
